{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>My heart for Moab.</b> On account of Moab." "<b>Her bars.</b> The bars of the city cry out, like 'wail, O gate' (Isaiah 14:31), or 'her bars' were brought to Egypt, for from there the king of Assyria came, or Zoar of Sodom, and 'the third heifer' that also has two, is an adjective for Zoar, and it is possible that בריחיה is an adjective like the sense of בורחיה, but בריחיה cannot be said, for how can a passive participle stand for an active participle?" "<b>יעוערו.</b> The second ayin is doubled [with dagesh] and the meaning is like עוררו (rouse/awaken), and it is a foreign word."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {1 "The text contrasts בריחיה (her bars) with בורחיה (her fugitives); Ibn Ezra notes the latter reading would require an active participle, which the passive form cannot yield. Also, 'the third heifer' translates עגלת שלישיה from the same verse (Isaiah 15:5), linked here to צוער (Zoar)."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:53:58.458979238Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}